Backport of saltstack/salt#66899 ("Fix Python 3.13 compatibility regarding
urllib.parse module") to salt 3006. Despite the upstream title naming
3.13, the same urllib.parse change was backported into recent Python 3.12
point releases, so the bug also reproduces there (see saltstack/salt#68080,
the downstream report on 3.12).

Root cause (cpython#85110): for relative paths, urllib.parse now builds
`urlunparse(("file", "", path, "", "", ""))` as `file:path` instead of
`file:///path`. `salt.utils.url.create` did:

    url = urlunparse(("file", "", path, "", query, ""))
    return "salt://{}".format(url[len("file:///") :])

i.e. blindly stripped 8 chars assuming `file:///` was always the prefix.
With the new behavior the prefix is shorter, so the first bytes of the
real path get eaten, e.g. `salt://services/foo` → `salt://vices/foo` and
`file.managed` / `cp.cache_file` then fail with
"Source file salt://... not found in saltenv 'base'".

Empirically, on Python 3.12 the bug only triggers when `cffi` is also
installed (via `cryptography`, which salt depends on). The mechanism is
not documented upstream; the downstream reporter just observes that
adding cffi flips the behavior. Either way the fix is the same - use
`urlunsplit` with an empty scheme so no `file:///` prefix has to be
stripped at all.

Upstream fix lands in salt 3008 (Argon); not backported to 3006.x. Drop
this patch when bumping salt past 3008.

--- a/salt/utils/url.py
+++ b/salt/utils/url.py
@@ -4,7 +4,7 @@

 import re
 import sys
-from urllib.parse import quote, urlparse, urlunparse
+from urllib.parse import quote, urlparse, urlunparse, urlunsplit

 import salt.utils.data
 import salt.utils.path
@@ -45,8 +45,9 @@ def create(path, saltenv=None):
     path = salt.utils.data.decode(path)

     query = f"saltenv={saltenv}" if saltenv else ""
-    url = salt.utils.data.decode(urlunparse(("file", "", path, "", query, "")))
-    return "salt://{}".format(url[len("file:///") :])
+    return "salt://{}".format(
+        salt.utils.data.decode(urlunsplit(("", "", path, query, "")))
+    )


 def is_escaped(url):
